  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Unlocking Zimbabwe?s Organic Potential - climate change addendum</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Zimbabwe imports organic products from neighbouring countries to meet growing domestic demand; at the same time 75% of Zimbabweans who rely on agriculture as their main source of income are barely able to produce at subsistence level and many are forced to barter for food. The vital knowledge of natural agricultural practices and pool of labour experienced to produce organically has been reduced by mass migration to cities and neighbouring countries caused by poverty.  Costly chemical approaches to farming are ill suited to the poor conditions experienced by farmers on communal lands and further reduce soil fertility and increase susceptibility of crops to pests and drought. This project will support resource-poor farmers to produce more crops without dependence on chemicals. It will also support the farmers to form farmer associations through which they can share learning, work together to overcome barriers to market access, and raise their issues with policy makers, charities, buyers and the media. Activities undertaken by the local partners will increase the awareness of consumers, institutional buyers, the media and policy makers of the positive links between organic agriculture and climate change mitigation. They will also put in place measures to link farmers to markets and remove potential barriers. The project will not only increase household food security and income of the targeted farmers mentioned above but also farmers trained during a pilot project and other small scale farmers across Zimbabwe. In addition, the project will increase demand for organic products, improve farmer&apos;s cash flow and market linkages, and galvanise government support for the sector.</narrative>
  </description>
